ARM64 trampoline backport validation
===================================

Host instruction tests:

  python3 tools/testing/selftests/bpf/test_arm64_trampoline.py

Requires Python 3 and a host C compiler (HOSTCC defaults to cc). The test
extracts the actual trampoline generator, JIT prologue, text-poke routine,
and ARM64 instruction encoders from this source tree. It executes generated
instructions in a small interpreter with ABI-conforming caller-clobbered
register destruction at every external call.

Coverage includes fentry argument preservation, fexit return contexts,
fmod_ret zero/nonzero chains, IP context, scalar widths, 0 through 11 argument
boundary cases including stack arguments, sleepable and non-sleepable hooks,
interpreted and JIT program dispatch, trampoline reference enter/exit,
detach-time fexit bypass, JIT tail-call prologue offsets, and CALL/JUMP
install/replace/remove instruction validation and branch-range checks.

The host test stubs RCU, synchronization, symbol lookup, and text writes.
It cannot validate kernel concurrency, cache maintenance, actual attach
verification, or device execution. Do not report its results as a device
selftest pass.

Device validation still required on the patched ARM64 kernel:

* Load fentry/fexit programs against an eligible BTF kernel function and
  against a BPF program/subprogram; verify arguments and return values.
* Attach multiple fmod_ret programs to an ALLOW_ERROR_INJECTION target.
  Verify zero continues, nonzero skips the original, and fexit sees the
  selected return value. The existing verifier controls eligible targets.
* Load a BPF_PROG_TYPE_EXT replacement with matching BTF, execute the target,
  detach, and verify the original result is restored. Repeat replace/detach.
* Run attach/update/detach while the original function sleeps and while
  other CPUs invoke it. Repeat with BPF statistics enabled and disabled,
  and with normal function/function-graph tracing enabled.
* Re-run existing JIT, BPF-to-BPF call, tail-call, and line-info tests.

ABI scope: the BTF model in this tree describes scalar/pointer arguments
(up to 11) and scalar returns up to 64 bits. Aggregate, floating-point,
and variadic calling conventions are not added by this backport. Generated
trampolines must fit the core's one-page allocation. Text changes outside
ARM64's +/-128 MiB direct-branch range return -ERANGE without patching code.
Kernel-function tracing requires DYNAMIC_FTRACE_WITH_REGS; BPF JIT text
patches do not depend on enabling function tracing.
